# UR: Consistent Core and MCP flow from artefact recording to closeout

Status: draft
Registration: renewed canonical UR draft; new deliberate approval required
Gate: UR
Gate approval: open
Requirements clarification: complete
Date: 2026-10-11
Supersedes sources at revision: 089a504e-29b4-42a9-8b1c-3558cfa34996
Owner: Arndt Gold
Run: core-mcp-flow-coherence-20261010-01
Language: en

## 1. Problem

The user asked the agent to record places where delivery requires code searches or direct Core calls as MCP candidates. Two cockpit runs exposed repeated searches for authoring formats and writer arguments, inconsistent next steps after approvals, manual structured closeout work and ambiguous current versus historical evidence. The user explicitly wants a coherent architecture and flow rather than isolated fixes that accumulate conflicting responsibilities.

The requested architecture assessment was created outside AGDF in `docs/reviews/ARCHITEKTUR_FLOW_MCP_KANDIDATEN_20261010.md`. It groups all recorded candidates into thirteen groups and compares them with existing architecture and source owners. It identifies four related weaknesses: incomplete state preparation in writers compared with readers; description text used as an execution condition; incomplete structured closeout and completed-state projection; and authoring/evidence contracts that require avoidable source inspection. Existing typed approval commands and document-state projections provide reusable owners, but their existence alone does not complete the lifecycle through MCP.

These findings are source analysis and historical execution observations. The assessment did not reproduce the failures through new runtime tests. Some candidates are already implemented or are ordinary engineering inspection rather than missing product APIs. Treating every candidate as a new MCP tool would create competing policy and duplicate functionality.

The requested integration of the decision card exposed a further concrete gap: this active Run has an approved UR and PRD and a linked, unapproved SD, but no supported operation can reopen the approved UR at that boundary. Draft-authoring revision, early PRD reopening and later CD+Tests source revision do not cover it. Repository and installed-runtime inspection confirm the restriction. Returning only the current SD status hides the missing capability. Users need a controlled way to change the earliest approved source within the same Run before implementation, preserving history and obtaining renewed decisions instead of creating a replacement Run solely to work around this gap.

The native qualification also exposed a connection whose required UI capability is unavailable, repeated host restarts and ambiguity about multiple server processes. The user requests a bounded replacement arrangement that retires the oldest eligible unused server. Multiple processes may belong to different chats; age alone establishes neither ownership nor abandonment. The existing diagnostic evidence does not prove that old servers cause the missing capability. This renewed source separates that unresolved native diagnosis from the requested lifecycle improvement.

## 2. Goal

Provide one bounded, coherent delivery flow from recording artefacts and evidence through presentation, deliberate human approval, permitted revision and governance closeout. Core, CLI, MCP and the relevant cockpit views must describe the same bound current state and the same permitted next operation. Routine use of supported operations must not require manual control-table edits or source searches to discover input formats.

MCP makes qualified existing Core capabilities available through explicit contracts. The app consumes the resulting current state, document identity, evidence and next-step descriptions. Human decisions remain deliberate and bound; governance completion remains separate from Git or publication actions. A focused chat decision card offers document-specific approval, request change and rejection, exact larger-view document inspection and discussion before deciding. The existing exact Approval formula remains a fully supported alternative, including later in the same conversation while the presentation remains current and eligible.

Extend the canonical revision capability to the explicitly bounded approved-UR reopening at SD described below. Core owns eligibility, impact, history and state transition; CLI and MCP expose that same operation and the app displays its result.

Permit controlled replacement of explicitly owned server generations without losing active views, replaying decisions or affecting another chat. Establish the connection lifecycle owner before designing this behavior; native capability and closure claims require actual host evidence. Retain existing code and tests as historical work requiring renewed plan fulfillment, rather than treating the reopened sources as a completed implementation.

## Affected Users

- Users directing delivery who need a understandable current state, current decision evidence and a reliable next step.
- Coding agents consuming AGDF contracts, including models that benefit from less schema discovery and fewer contradictory results.
- Reviewers and operators verifying revisions, artefact identity, recovery, approvals and completed-run/backlog consistency.

Improved performance of weaker models is an expected benefit to evaluate, not an already measured fact or a guarantee for every model and host.

## 3. Scope

This is one integrated increment aligned with the existing R-04 MCP lifecycle direction. Its dependent implementation stages must share one design and end-to-end acceptance plan.

1. **Consistent state and continuation:** Establish complete, consistent preparation of persisted and proposed state in existing Core readers and writers, including internal analyses, UX requirements, source revisions and registered artefacts. Derive the current next operation from canonical evaluation. Technical routing must use stable machine-readable meaning rather than an exact human-facing sentence. A write effect and its receipt remain distinguishable from a fresh current-state observation.
2. **Usable authoring and operation contracts:** Describe supported inputs, effects, applicable prerequisites, authoring forms and concrete validation failures through their existing owners. Authoring checks, criterion interpretation and presentation preparation must agree on supported forms. Descriptions must match the executing version and distinguish unsupported capabilities, malformed inputs, unavailable checks and blocked prerequisites. Include only owner/check orientation necessary to perform this flow.
3. **Existing recording, presentation, approval and revision capabilities:** Connect the existing writers for internal analyses, artefact/evidence registration, review/QA recording, presentation preparation, deliberate approval recording and supported source revision, including the bounded extension in item 8. Preserve required registration/seal ordering and dependencies. Revision previews and applications remain separate; lifecycle cases outside the declared extension remain explicitly unsupported. Reuse the existing typed approval command rather than adding another approval authority. Describe and qualify retry, payload conflict, concurrency and interrupted-operation behavior for each exposed write.
4. **Structured governance closeout:** Provide a bounded closeout path for the existing structured delivery modes, alongside compatible Quick Task behavior. OR, lifecycle completion and backlog pointers must yield a consistent completed state and recoverable interrupted synchronization. A completed run with satisfied closeout requirements must not ask for the same OR again. Missing or invalid requirements remain visible. Governance completion does not execute or require a commit, push or publication.
5. **Current evidence and appropriate app display:** Derive current QA/UAT and decision evidence from available exact bindings; distinguish superseded history and unresolved association. Preserve the implemented document-state, exact readable-version and bound draft-check behavior. The cockpit must present the same current Core interpretation, useful next-step descriptions and truthful document/evidence links. It remains a consumer of Core policy and never evaluates gates independently. A narrowly scoped decision card adds explicit deliberate human actions through the existing approval authority; it does not turn the general read-only cockpit into an arbitrary writer.
6. **MCP exposure and whole-flow qualification:** Expose the qualified flow through appropriate MCP contracts backed by the same owners used by CLI. Define compatibility for existing consumers before changing them. Test the complete chain, negative paths and recovery; assess whether supported routine operations still require undocumented Core calls or control edits. Record actual package/runtime/resource identity and host observations where needed to substantiate a claim. Source, build, protocol, browser and native host evidence remain distinct.

7. **Deliberate decision card with equal text route:** Present a concise complete decision summary, relevant changes since the prior applicable version or honest unavailable comparison, open issues, exact document link and effect/next step. Offer approve, request change with comment and reject when supported by the current gate. Users may inspect the document in a larger supported view, return and discuss with the AI. Discussion alone records no approval; changing material/control requires a fresh presentation. Keep exact Approval: UR/PRD/SD/TP/QA/UAT as an equal route wherever that gate applies. Human click origin, host tool permission and canonical approval effect are distinct. Progress precedes confirmed success; stale/expired/replaced cards cannot approve, historical decisions stay inspectable, lost acknowledgements and concurrent click/text require receipt-based resolution without duplicate effects. Reuse existing presentation/approval/document/session owners and qualify the actual Codex card, larger view, user interaction and result against one exact candidate. Text fallback alone is not native click evidence.

8. **Controlled approved-UR reopening at SD:** Support an active structured Run at SD with approved UR/PRD and linked unapproved SD, before SD/TP approval, implementation or closeout. A deliberate request to change the UR obtains a read-only impact preview and a separately bound application through the canonical revision/transaction/history owners. Check exact target, Run, revision, source identities and preview before applying. Preserve the exact affected UR/PRD/SD, analyses, approvals, presentations, mappings and receipts as history; invalidate affected current approvals and dependent bindings without transferring decisions or deleting history. Reassess Brownfield/UX applicability and return this same Run to UR drafting, registration and a fresh UR decision, followed by renewed dependent PRD/SD decisions and the normal TP/implementation gates. Run and backlog must agree or expose a recoverable interruption. Core, CLI, MCP and dispatcher must describe the supported boundary and concrete rejection reason rather than silently returning an unrelated SD approval request. No second revision policy, generic maintenance bypass or reopening of arbitrary later lifecycle states is added.

The assessment's main groups K-01 to K-06, K-11 and K-12 define the integration problem. K-07 supplies protected implemented behavior. K-08/K-09 enter only where needed to use or qualify this flow. K-10 is deferred test infrastructure; K-13 remains normal engineering inspection. These group labels are assessment references, not new normative operation IDs.

9. **Bounded connection-owned server replacement:** Identify the actual lifecycle controller for each host connection before selecting an implementation. A qualified new generation may accept new views only after confirmed startup, matching runtime/resource identity and genuinely advertised required capability. Existing active views and in-flight decisions remain with their original generation and operation identity. Retire the oldest explicitly owned generation that has no active views, running command or unresolved effect through its orderly close path with confirmed termination; age is only an ordering criterion among eligible generations. Define a finite capacity and refuse further replacement visibly when all slots are active or not safely closable. Failed startup/capability qualification preserves the old usable connection; a failed retirement remains visible and consumes capacity until resolved. Concurrent replacements cannot produce competing current generations. Do not replay a business command, migrate an uncertain decision, evict another chat or infer ownership from PID, process name, repository path or age. Brownfield/PRD must resolve which parts are owned by AGDF and which require Codex-host support; an unavailable external owner is an explicit dependency/blocker, not permission for a local workaround. Concrete capacity, drain/expiry limits and error presentation are deferred PRD/SD decisions with that owner. Diagnose the actual missing native capability separately; a pool is not evidence of restored UI support. This is a bounded extension of the same coherent flow, not a general orchestration platform.

## 4. Non-Goals

- One new tool for each candidate, a universal code-understanding service, a general orchestration store or a second policy/approval authority. Only the bounded connection lifecycle in scope 9 is added; its owner must avoid a parallel governance source of truth.
- Automatic gate approvals, inferred human decisions, removed gates, weakened source seals or unbound target/run/revision operations.
- Stronger independent host enforcement or independent human-response proof merely because an operation is reachable through MCP. Existing cooperative assurance must be described honestly.
- General owner/test inventory, synthetic product-facing test sessions, installer migration, blanket automatic reconnection/restart and qualification of all hosts/models. Only explicitly owned, bounded generation replacement in scope 9 is included. No automatic decision replay, global PID ring, foreign-chat eviction or modification of installed Codex binaries. Narrow diagnostics and fixtures necessary for this flow remain separated from productive operations; native qualification cannot be replaced by remote iPad confirmation or synthetic tests.
- A general cockpit redesign, arbitrary app writer access or reimplementation of the completed document-state and draft-check slices. Only the explicit bound gate-decision interaction is added.
- Changing Request Activation, treating read-only questions as delivery authority, silently editing approved or foreign runs, or transferring their approvals.
- Commit, push, release, deployment or publication; completing unrelated active runs.

## 5. Acceptance Signals

- **AC-001 — One current meaning:** For the same bound source/run revision, writer follow-up, Inspect, dispatcher, CLI/MCP results, backlog interpretation and cockpit agree on current gate, satisfied prerequisites, permitted operation and completion. Cover a renewed source with a registered completed UX analysis and subsequent PRD, SD, QA and UAT approvals, plus a flow without source revision. Historical receipts do not overwrite current observations.
- **AC-002 — Semantic routing:** Changing equivalent display wording does not change technical continuation. Stable operation meaning and its prerequisites come from the owning Core contract; adapters and app introduce no alternate operation-policy table. Stale or ambiguous state remains rejected or explicitly unresolved.
- **AC-003 — Authoring usability:** Defined valid UR/PRD/SD/TP examples are accepted consistently by readiness and presentation preparation. Invalid supported-input examples identify the affected source/field/form and applicable correction. Capability descriptions reflect actual executing behavior. Performing these supported examples does not require reading parser implementation to discover required syntax.
- **AC-004 — Bound recording and decisions:** An end-to-end flow records analyses, artefacts, evidence and reviews in the required order, prepares a bound presentation and records a new deliberate click or exact text response through the same existing approval authority. Revision uses supported preview/application behavior. No routine hand-edit of control tables is required; malformed, unsupported, foreign or stale operations cannot manufacture review completion, QA success or approval.
- **AC-005 — Recovery and concurrency:** For each exposed write, documented and tested behavior covers lost acknowledgement, identical retry, conflicting payload under the same operation identity where applicable, concurrent run/source changes and interrupted persistence. Receipts/current state or a concrete recovery condition resolve uncertainty. Unsupported repeatability is explicit; a uniform API must not claim idempotence absent implementation evidence.
- **AC-006 — Complete closeout:** Quick Task and both existing structured modes distinguish fulfilled OR/completed lifecycle from missing or invalid closeout. OR registration, lifecycle and backlog-pointer synchronization remain consistent or visibly recoverable after interruption. Completed runs produce no repeated closeout requirement, and no VCS action occurs as a consequence of completion.
- **AC-007 — Truthful evidence and app:** Current QA/UAT decision material is associated with the applicable source/revision where that association can be established. Superseded history is distinguished; unstructured unknown association is shown honestly. Existing exact-version links and bound draft findings retain their semantics. Refresh, stale responses and relevant view/session lifecycle cases do not introduce another app policy or revive outdated results. Changed localized presentations are checked in every registered language.
- **AC-008 — Real transport parity and compatibility:** Actual MCP calls and the corresponding Core/CLI paths have matching semantic results for the defined positive, invalid-input, stale-binding and recovery scenarios. Existing readable contracts/receipts remain compatible or have an explicit qualified transition plan. Report precisely which runtime, resource and host path was exercised; do not claim unobserved native behavior or stronger response assurance.
- **AC-009 — Integrated delivery and benefit:** One approved design resolves state ownership, operation contracts, authoring/evidence interpretation, closeout and compatibility before implementation. Dependent stages cannot be declared complete while another consumer still contradicts the same current state. A fixed before/after scenario set records required search/manual steps, schema failures, wrong follow-ups, redundant continuation prompts and successful recovery separately from necessary approvals and external actions. Model benefit is reported only at the level actually measured.

- **AC-010 — Read, discuss and decide:** A focused decision-ready card explains the exact document, material version changes/open issues and approval consequence. Its document opens in the larger supported view with return to the decision. Three actions approve, request a concrete change or reject this version through existing owners; reading, discussion or view closure do not decide. The exact Approval text remains available later for the still-current eligible presentation; quoted/example/negated/stale/foreign/future-gate responses are not accepted.
- **AC-011 — Deliberate origin and truthful result:** Human interaction and host permission are distinguished within the actual supported assurance boundary; agent invocation, model flags and hostile document/tool content alone cannot establish a human decision. Core target/Run/gate/revision/document/presentation checks remain. Progress then confirmed effect/next action, or explicit uncertainty/status/recovery, cover double-click, concurrent text/click, lost acknowledgement, source changes and expired/replaced cards without duplicate decisions.
- **AC-012 — Actual accessible card qualification:** Exact native Codex inline card, larger document view, actual user action, registered result and affected interaction/teardown are evidenced separately from source/build/protocol tests. Keyboard, focus, narrow/wide layouts and all registered languages are checked. Unsupported host capability stays explicit; text fallback remains usable but cannot substitute for the new native-click acceptance.
- **AC-013 — Same-Run approved-UR revision before implementation:** Starting from an active structured SD Run with approved UR/PRD and linked unapproved SD, an explicit UR change produces a read-only impact preview; applying its exact current binding preserves affected history, invalidates affected current decisions/bindings and returns the same Run to a fresh UR decision with consistent Run/backlog state. Reassessment and renewed dependent approvals are required; old approval replies cannot authorize the replacement. Actual Core/CLI/MCP paths agree on eligibility and effects. Foreign/stale bindings, changed previews, payload conflicts, concurrent changes, repeated application and interruptions during archive/state/backlog persistence yield the documented rejection, receipt or recovery outcome without duplicate effects or lost history. Lifecycle cases beyond this boundary remain explicitly rejected. Qualification includes the concrete decision-card scope change that exposed this gap and requires neither a substitute Run nor manual control-table edits.

- **AC-014 — Explicit lifecycle ownership:** Before implementation, the same design identifies the actual connection/process, view/session and command owners, their permitted control surfaces and finite capacity. Ownership prevents foreign-chat/connection termination and a parallel policy/state store. If host support is necessary and unavailable, the relevant native outcome stays explicitly blocked; no repository-only simulation claims to control the host.
- **AC-015 — Qualified replacement and protected decisions:** A successful replacement qualifies the new generation before routing new views; the old active view remains readable until its own end. Failed startup, identity or UI capability keeps the original usable path. Simultaneous replacements yield one current generation. In-flight or lost-acknowledgement approvals retain their original operation UUID, receipt/current-state resolution or explicit uncertainty, without automatic resubmission, duplicate decision or migration of an unknown effect. Multiple-chat tests show no foreign termination; age alone never evicts an active server.
- **AC-016 — Bounded retirement and native proof:** The oldest eligible own idle generation closes with explicit end evidence and no remaining owned worker/transport reader in the confirmed closed state. Retirement failure remains visible and capacity-accounted. At full capacity with active or unresolved generations, replacement is rejected with an actionable explanation and the existing sessions preserved. Restart/expiry/interruption follow documented bounded recovery. Actual host initialization/resource identity, visible inline/larger view, interaction/focus and teardown evidence qualify the exact candidate separately from source/build/protocol tests. Missing native capability remains unresolved until that evidence exists; the server pool does not imply its repair.

## 6. Existing Source Of Truth

- This conversation: the candidate-recording request, concern about uncontrolled individual fixes, request for an architecture/flow assessment outside AGDF, agreement to one coherent run and the instruction to create this UR. Historical gate approvals in the conversation do not approve this renewed scope.
- Subsequent instructions in this conversation: integrate the decision card into this existing Run, retain the exact Approval text during discussion, and record the currently unsupported approved-UR reopening route as an explicit UR requirement. These instructions define this renewed UR. The user separately authorized the bounded Core revision bootstrap outside AGDF; that authorization is not a gate approval or authority to implement the remaining integrated scope.
- `docs/reviews/ARCHITEKTUR_FLOW_MCP_KANDIDATEN_20261010.md`: non-normative assessment of all candidate groups, source-confirmed causes, proposed stages and unresolved design decisions. It is input to this run, not approved implementation design.
- `.agdf/control/artefacts/cockpit-draft-check-integration-20261009-01/MCP_CANDIDATES.md` and `.agdf/control/artefacts/cockpit-documented-approvals-20261009-01/MCP_CANDIDATES.md`: historical observations and later corrections; their approved artefacts and completed scope remain protected.
- `docs/architecture/03-agentenkontrolle-zielbild.md`: existing ownership/operation catalogue and R-04/R-05 distinction. `04-mcp-schnittstellen.md`, `02-dispatcher.md` and `06-agdf-cockpit.md` describe existing transport, continuation and app boundaries.
- `packages/core/lib/control-evaluation/`, `control-state/`, `control-inspect/` and `skill-dispatch/`: canonical evaluation, state preparation, authoring readiness, source/proof/evidence, writers, typed approval, presentation, recovery and continuation owners. `packages/cli/lib/mcp-dispatch-runtime.js` and `packages/mcp-server/` transport these capabilities; the app consumes Core projections.
- Related active scopes include `gate-internal-continuation-recovery-20261008-01`, `status-card-stale-next-action-20261008-01`, `backlog-status-flow-clarity-20261009-01`, `agdf-intake-continuation-repair` and the concept-only `agent-control-dispatcher-mcp-concept-20261002-01`. Brownfield Review must establish actual overlap and reuse against their current baseline. This run owns the integrated operational contract and flow; it neither silently absorbs their obligations nor edits or closes them.

- `SERVER_LIFECYCLE_CHANGE_PROPOSAL.md` and `evidence/SERVER_LIFECYCLE_REVISION_PREVIEW_01.json` record the reviewed change and canonical same-Run revision impact. The source revision operation `fb62a4b9-61fb-4d55-a864-d3ed9c86fa4d` archives superseded UR/PRD/SD/TP and resets affected approvals/fulfillment; it preserves code/tests/history and approves no renewed source.
- `packages/mcp-server/src/main.js`, `server.js` and `worker.js` own the current per-STDIO runtime/executor shutdown; `packages/core/lib/control-inspect/decision-session.js` owns bounded view sessions. These are different lifecycle boundaries, not evidence of a cross-process host controller. `evidence/NATIVE_CONNECTION_FINDING_01.md`, `NATIVE_CAPABILITY_OBSERVER_CONFIGURATION_01.json` and `PARALLEL_READER_REGRESSION_01.json` separate outstanding actual native connection proof from qualified configuration/reader work. Existing native milestones are historical candidate-bound evidence, not approval or native qualification of this extension.

## 7. Risks And Unknowns

### Server Lifecycle Risks And Deferred Decisions

- Brownfield Review and PRD must establish whether the required process control belongs to the external Codex host or an explicitly ownable repository launch boundary. Multiple processes and missing UI capability do not establish a common cause. If no supported control surface can deliver the requested replacement, report the external prerequisite and affected acceptance rather than constructing a process killer.
- The connection owner must define capacity, admission, generation identity, drain/expiry and close acknowledgement in PRD/SD. Finite limits must protect active views and uncertain command effects; this UR does not authorize changing approval retry or effect semantics. Tool access and host connection permission remain separate from business approval.
- Human native keyboard/layout/closure observations require the actual tested Mac host and candidate. The user currently operates from iPad; remote chat approvals can continue, but do not qualify unobserved Mac UI behavior. QA and full flow/review obligations stay open and require renewed TP evidence.

### Retained Integrated Flow Risks

- The assessment explains source paths but contains no fresh reproduction. Brownfield Review must reproduce relevant inconsistencies, distinguish already repaired behavior and select a proportionate delivery mode/slice. Shared active-owner changes require explicit overlap handling rather than competing implementations.
- PRD must define supported scenarios, user-facing states and benefit measurement. SD must decide which existing state-preparation entry is shared, typed operation meaning and compatibility, canonical authoring/criterion interpretation, evidence applicability, existing approval-route convergence and operation-specific retry/recovery. Exact APIs, field names and tool count are not settled by this UR.
- Closeout involves multiple durable records. SD must specify consistency, interruption detection and recovery rather than assuming multi-file atomicity. Completion must not erase remaining obligations or become publication authority.
- Free historical evidence may lack enough identity for automatic applicability. Unknown applicability must remain explicit; migration must not fabricate bindings or rewrite historical approvals.
- Cooperative human-response provenance and native-host constraints remain real limits. Qualification must bind observations to the actual tested candidate and avoid repeating broad host checks without a changed requirement or candidate.
- Reduced search overhead is an expected outcome. Normal code review and architecture inspection remain necessary; fewer calls achieved by weakening validation do not demonstrate improvement.

- The accepted decision-card concept and equal text approval are integrated into this same Run. Scope 8 and AC-013 define the early approved-UR revision requirement. A separately user-authorized bounded Core bootstrap implements that boundary through the existing revision/history/transaction owners, with local Core/CLI and candidate qualification recorded in CORE_REVISION_BOOTSTRAP_01.md. Canonical reopening preserves exact previous evidence and revokes affected current decisions; it does not approve this UR or authorize remaining implementation. Installed runtime/native-host claims remain separate. PRD/SD must use the qualified existing capability and specify the remaining integrated transport, diagnostic and UX behavior.
- SD must resolve real user-click provenance versus model-callable input within the actual host boundary; a nonce, UI message or generated Approval text does not itself prove independent human origin. Missing required host support blocks click qualification; cooperative limits remain explicit.

## 8. Next Step

Present this renewed canonical UR with its exact current revision and obtain a new deliberate `Approval: UR`. The bounded bootstrap enables same-Run revision and retains old sources/decisions as history; it grants no approval for this changed scope. Reassess Brownfield/UX applicability after UR approval, then renew dependent PRD/SD decisions and the normal TP, implementation and readiness steps. The prior approvals cannot authorize this replacement.

Include connection ownership, controlled server replacement and the unresolved native-capability cause in that reassessment. Reuse retained implementation/tests only after checking their current applicability. Do not request native Mac actions from an iPad-only session as if they had been performed.

## AGDF Approval Summary (de; source=en)

- Problem: Die Kandidatensammlungen zeigen widersprüchliche Folgeschritte nach Freigaben, vermeidbare Suche nach Autorenformaten und Writer-Aufrufen, manuelle strukturierte Abschlüsse sowie unklare aktuelle und historische Nachweise. Einzelne neue MCP-Tools würden diese Ursachen nicht gemeinsam lösen. Die Architektur-/Flow-Bewertung wurde ohne AGDF erstellt und enthält Quellbefunde und historische Beobachtungen; sie ersetzt keine neue Laufzeitprüfung.
- Ziel und Betroffene: Nutzer, Agenten und Reviewer erhalten einen konsistenten Ablauf von Artefakt- und Nachweiserfassung über Darstellung, bewusste Freigabe und erlaubte Revision bis zum Governance-Abschluss. Core, CLI, MCP und Cockpit beschreiben denselben gebundenen aktuellen Zustand und erlaubten nächsten Vorgang. Weniger Suchaufwand soll auch schwächeren Modellen helfen; dieser Nutzen ist noch zu messen.
- Gemeinsamer Umfang: Vollständige Zustandsaufbereitung und maschinenlesbare Fortsetzung im Core; Autoren-/Operationsverträge mit konkreten Fehlerhinweisen; Verbindung vorhandener Writer für Analysen, Artefakte, Reviews, QA, Darstellung, Freigabe und unterstützte Revision. Wiederholung, Konkurrenz und Unterbrechungen werden je Schreibvorgang geklärt. Ein strukturierter Abschluss verbindet OR, Lebenszyklus und Backlog zuverlässig. MCP macht qualifizierte Core-Fähigkeiten zugänglich; die App zeigt deren aktuelle Ergebnisse und Nachweise. Eine begrenzte Entscheidungskarte ergänzt exakte Dokumentansicht, Diskussion und drei bewusste Aktionen; der vorhandene Textweg bleibt gleichwertig. Umsetzung erfolgt in abhängigen Etappen mit gemeinsamem Entwurf und Gesamtprüfung.
- AC-001: Writer, Inspect, Dispatcher, CLI/MCP, Backlog und Cockpit stimmen bei derselben Revision überein, auch nach Quellenrevision und erledigter UX-Analyse. Historische Belege bleiben von der aktuellen Lage unterscheidbar.
- AC-002: Andere gleichbedeutende Anzeigeformulierungen ändern keine technische Fortsetzung. Operationsbedeutung und Voraussetzungen gehören dem Core; veraltete oder unklare Bindungen bleiben abgewiesen oder ausdrücklich ungeklärt.
- AC-003: Definierte gültige UR-/PRD-/SD-/TP-Beispiele bestehen Autorenprüfung und Präsentationsvorbereitung übereinstimmend. Fehler nennen Quelle, Feld oder Form und passende Korrektur. Unterstützte Syntax ist ohne Suche im Parser nutzbar; Beschreibungen passen zur ausführenden Version.
- AC-004: Der Gesamtflow erfasst Analysen, Artefakte und Reviews in richtiger Reihenfolge, bereitet die gebundene Darstellung vor und erfasst eine neue bewusste Antwort über die vorhandene Freigabefunktion. Unterstützte Revision nutzt Vorschau und Anwendung. Routine-Handedits entfallen; ungültige Aufrufe erzeugen keine Freigabe oder QA-Aufwertung.
- AC-005: Jeder exponierte Schreibvorgang besitzt belegtes Verhalten für verlorene Antworten, Wiederholung, Nutzlastkonflikte soweit anwendbar, konkurrierende Änderungen und unterbrochene Speicherung. Beleg, aktuelle Lage oder konkrete Recovery lösen Unsicherheit; unbelegte Idempotenz wird nicht behauptet.
- AC-006: Quick Task und beide strukturierten Modi unterscheiden abgeschlossenen Governance-Stand von fehlendem OR. OR, Lebenszyklus und Backlog bleiben konsistent oder sichtbar wiederherstellbar. Ein erledigter Abschluss verlangt keinen weiteren OR und führt keine Git-Aktion aus.
- AC-007: Aktuelle QA-/UAT-Entscheidungsnachweise werden aus verfügbaren exakten Bindungen abgeleitet; ersetzte Historie und unbekannte Zuordnung bleiben erkennbar. Dokumentfassungen und gebundene Entwurfsbefunde behalten ihre Bedeutung. Refresh und Lebenszyklusfälle verwenden dieselbe Core-Policy; geänderte Darstellungen werden in allen registrierten Sprachen geprüft.
- AC-008: Echte MCP-Aufrufe entsprechen Core/CLI für positive, ungültige, veraltete und Recovery-Fälle. Bestehende Verträge bleiben kompatibel oder erhalten einen qualifizierten Übergang. Laufzeit-, Ressourcen- und Hostnachweise benennen die tatsächlich geprüfte Ausführung; keine unbeobachteten Host- oder stärkeren Herkunftszusagen.
- AC-009: Ein gemeinsamer freigegebener Entwurf klärt Eigentümer, Verträge, Formate, Nachweise, Abschluss und Kompatibilität vor Umsetzung. Keine Etappe ist fertig, solange andere Konsumenten widersprechen. Feste Vorher-/Nachher-Fälle messen Such-/Handschritte, Schemafehler, falsche Folgeangaben, unnötige Fortsetzungsimpulse und Recovery getrennt von notwendigen Entscheidungen und externen Aktionen.
- AC-010: Aktuelle Karte mit vollständiger Kurzfassung, relevanten Änderungen/offenen Punkten und Entscheidungswirkung; exaktes Dokument groß öffnen und zurückkehren. Freigeben, Änderung mit Kommentar und Ablehnen folgen bestehenden Owners. Diskussion bleibt möglich; die passende exakte Approval-Zeile kann später die noch aktuelle entscheidungsbereite Fassung freigeben. Fragen/Zitate/Beispiele/alte Antworten erteilen keine Freigabe.
- AC-011: Nutzerklick, Tool-Erlaubnis und Core-Freigabe sind getrennt. Keine modellgenerierte Entscheidung. Erst bestätigter Effekt bedeutet Erfolg; Doppelklick, gleichzeitige Text-/Klickantwort, verlorene Antwort und veraltete/abgelaufene Karten erhalten belegten Status/Recovery ohne doppelte Freigabe.
- AC-012: Exakte tatsächliche Codex-Karte, große Dokumentansicht, echte Nutzerhandlung und registriertes Ergebnis werden samt betroffenen Tastatur-/Fokus-/Layout-/Teardown-Fällen und allen Sprachen nachgewiesen. Textfallback bleibt erhalten, ersetzt aber keinen Klicknachweis.
- AC-013: Im aktiven strukturierten Run am SD-Gate mit freigegebener UR/PRD und verknüpfter, noch nicht freigegebener SD wird eine UR-Änderung im selben Run unterstützt. Vor Umsetzung: reine Auswirkungsvorschau, getrennte exakt gebundene Anwendung, Erhalt alter Fassungen und Belege, Entwertung betroffener aktueller Freigaben/Bindungen, erneute Analysen und neue UR-/PRD-/SD-Entscheidungen. Core/CLI/MCP stimmen überein; veraltete/fremde Aufrufe, Konkurrenz, Wiederholung und Unterbrechungen erhalten konkrete Fehler, Belege oder Recovery. Keine Freigabenübertragung, Ersatzruns oder Handedits; spätere Lebenszyklusfälle bleiben außerhalb dieser Erweiterung.
- Erweiterung Serveraustausch: Nur eindeutig eigene Generationen einer Host-Verbindung. Neue Ansichten wechseln erst nach erfolgreicher Initialisierung, passender Runtime-/Ressourcenidentität und tatsächlich deklarierter UI-Fähigkeit. Aktive Ansichten und laufende Entscheidungen bleiben beim bisherigen Besitzer. Der älteste sicher unbenutzte eigene Server wird geordnet beendet. Eine feste Kapazität verhindert unbegrenztes Wachstum; bei vollständig aktiver oder ungewisser Belegung wird ein weiterer Wechsel sichtbar abgelehnt.
- AC-014: Brownfield/PRD klären Prozess-, Ansichts- und Auftragsbesitzer sowie die echte Steuerungsmöglichkeit vor Umsetzung. Fremde Chats bleiben geschützt; nötige, fehlende Codex-Host-Unterstützung wird als externe Abhängigkeit ausgewiesen.
- AC-015: Neue Generation vor neuem Verkehr qualifizieren; Fehler erhalten die bisherige nutzbare Verbindung. Gleichzeitige Wechsel erzeugen keine konkurrierenden aktuellen Server. Laufende oder ungewisse Freigaben behalten UUID und belegten Effekt/Recovery; kein automatisches Wiederholen und keine doppelte Entscheidung. Kein fremder oder aktiver Server wird wegen seines Alters beendet.
- AC-016: Geordnetes Ende des ältesten geeigneten eigenen Servers mit Nachweis; Schließfehler belegen weiter Kapazität. Vollbelegung führt zur erklärten Ablehnung ohne Sitzungsverlust. Tatsächliche native UI-Aushandlung, Karte, Dokument/Rückkehr, Fokus und Teardown am exakten Kandidaten bleiben eigenständige Nachweise. Der Pool beweist weder die Ursache noch die Behebung fehlender UI-Fähigkeit.
- Abgrenzung: Kein Tool je Kandidat, allgemeiner Orchestrator, zweite Policy oder automatische Freigabe. Keine Änderung der Request Activation, stillen Fremdrun-Edits, Genehmigungsübertragung oder pauschalen Host-/Modellzusagen. Allgemeines Diagnose-/Testinventar, synthetische Produkt-Testprofile, Installer-Migration, pauschale Neustarts und Git/Veröffentlichung bleiben außerhalb. Die begrenzte Serverersetzung ist jetzt enthalten; kein globaler PID-Ring, automatisches Wiederholen von Entscheidungen oder Ändern installierter Codex-Binärdateien. Bestehende Dokumentzustands-/Entwurfsprüfungen bleiben geschützt; keine allgemeine App-Schreibschnittstelle.
- Eigentümer und offene Entwurfsfragen: Das vorhandene Agentenkontroll-Zielbild und R-04 bleiben maßgeblich; R-05-Nachweise gelten nur für tatsächlich qualifizierte Hosts. Core-Reader, Autoren-/Proof-Prüfung und vorhandene Writer tragen die Semantik; Adapter transportieren, App zeigt an. Brownfield klärt Reproduktion, Wiederverwendung und Überschneidungen mit aktiven Runs. PRD/SD klären genaue Szenarien, API-/Operationsform, Kriterieninterpretation, Evidenzzuordnung, Kompatibilität und Recovery. Mehrdatei-Abschluss darf keine unbelegte Atomizität behaupten; freie historische Evidenz und kooperative Antwortherkunft bleiben ehrlich begrenzt.
- Eigentümerschaft und Nachweis der Erweiterung: Der aktuelle AGDF-STDIO-Startpfad schließt seine eigene Runtime und Worker; Ansichts-/Entscheidungssitzungen besitzen einen anderen Lebenszyklus. Eine generationsübergreifende Host-Steuerung ist damit noch nicht belegt. Brownfield/PRD klären AGDF versus Codex-Host; PRD/SD bestimmen Kapazität, Entleerung, Ablauf und Fehlerdarstellung. Die aktuelle fehlende UI-Fähigkeit bleibt gesondert zu diagnostizieren. Die Bedienung vom iPad erlaubt die Diskussion und Quellenfreigabe, ersetzt aber keine native Mac-Prüfung.
- Nächster Schritt: Diese erweiterte UR wird im selben Run neu registriert und vorgelegt. Die kontrollierte Quellenrevision hat bisherige UR-/PRD-/SD-/TP-Freigaben archiviert und zurückgesetzt; Code, Tests und Historie bleiben erhalten. Eine neue bewusste `Approval: UR` ist erforderlich. Danach folgen erneute Brownfield-/UX-Bewertung einschließlich Serverbesitzer und native Ursache sowie abhängige PRD-/SD-/TP-Entscheidungen. Umsetzung, Gesamtflow, Reviews und QA benötigen aktuelle Nachweise unter dem erneuerten TP. Installierte Laufzeit und native Host-Nachweise bleiben getrennt.
